---
/**
 * Kraft box size reference.
 *
 * The page ranked for roughly 648 impressions of size-related queries at an
 * average position of 28.7 and took no clicks from any of them, because it
 * carried no size information at all — `kraft box 8x10` alone is ~484
 * impressions at position 56.8.
 *
 * This is deliberately a reference, not a catalog. Every run is cut on a
 * fresh dieline around the customer's product, so a fixed size list would be
 * a lie about how the business works. There is no board column for the same
 * reason: board follows product weight and journey, not outside dimensions.
 */
import { SIZE_REFERENCE } from '../../data/home-content'
---

<section class="section sizes" aria-labelledby="sizes-heading">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section__head" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="sizes-heading">Kraft box sizes and how to choose one</h2>
      <p>
        Send us your product dimensions and we build the dieline around them, so
        these are reference sizes rather than a fixed catalog. They are useful
        starting points when working out what type of box your product needs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll sizes__scroll" data-reveal>
      <table class="spec-table spec-table--narrow">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Reference kraft box sizes and the products they typically suit
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Approx. size</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical products</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SIZE_REFERENCE.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.emphasis ? <strong>{r.size}</strong> : r.size}</th>
              <td>{r.products}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="sizes__notes" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Treat the table as reference points rather than a range you order from.
        Every box is manufactured around the actual dimensions of the product,
        so the figure that matters is what you measure, not the nearest row
        here.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the box takes an insert, allow for it separately — the insert sits
        between the product and the wall, and the box has to grow to carry it.
        How much depends on the insert material and how the product is held, so
        it is worked out per job rather than from a single clearance figure.
      </p>
      <p>
        For anything that ships on its own, keep empty volume out of the box.
        Couriers charge on dimensional weight, so a{' '}
        <a href="/product/kraft-mailer-boxes/">kraft mailer box</a> cut one size
        too large costs money on every parcel you send, not just the first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Final structure and material follow from three things: how heavy the
        product is, how much protection it needs, and how it is distributed.
        Send those and we specify the box; the{' '}
        <a href="/product-category/kraft-packaging-boxes/">kraft packaging boxes</a>{' '}
        range shows the formats those decisions usually land on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Room is reserved by the table's own row count rather than by a fixed
     height, so nothing shifts once the fonts land. */
  .sizes__scroll {
    margin-top: clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 1vw, 2rem);
    background: #fff;
  }

  .sizes__notes {
    margin: clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 1vw, 2rem) auto 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .sizes__notes p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .sizes__notes p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
